  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Physical Description
    
    A hand-span of shadow-steel with a core of captured fire that pulses faintly in darkness — visible only if one knows where to look. The blade tapers to a narrow point; the hilt is wrapped in ancestor-bone that retains warmth from the deep forges where it was made. A fracture runs the full length of the steel, hairline at the tang and widening toward the point to approximately the width of a shed pin-feather, glowing faintly orange where the crack refuses to close. The fracture is the blade's defining feature and, increasingly, its death.
    
    ## Origin and Early History
    
    Forged by the Veil-render during the Clever Ice Age, an era whose artifacts I have learned to handle with more caution than admiration. The blade was crafted from shadow-steel around a sealed fire-core — a technique the deep forges no longer reproduce, assuming anyone were left to attempt it. Veil-render Seciae~, a Nightshelf engineer, carried ☽'micseleia' in an ancestor-bone sheath during the founding expansion of ☽'scar Ramtatus~, a Nightshelf colony. The grip was not yet worn to Seciae~'s hand, which suggests the blade had few wielders before that period, or none.
    
    ## The Fracture
    
    ☽'Netemia Glow-bringer, a Nightshelf warrior of the Orca Incursion period, drove the dagger through a blood-ward meant to bind the orca-dead during the Circle Gathering at the Still Pools, a seasonal convocation site. The steel cracked from inside. This is the wound that defines the weapon's subsequent history: irreparable, worsening with each use, bleeding out the sealed fire that gives the blade its power.
    
    Four practitioners have attempted restoration. None succeeded. The Nightshelf Deep-Archive register compiled by Keeper-Registrar ☽'noctmuluevus 'born confirms the attempts and notes that the fracture widens with each use. What the restorations cost their practitioners is not recorded in any archive that admits to holding the information — a phrasing I recognize from Nightshelf record-keeping as meaning *the information exists and you will not be shown it*.
    
    ## Chain of Custody
    
    The blade's passage through hands is better documented than most Nightshelf artifacts, though "better" is relative.
    
    | **Holder** | **Context** | **Outcome** |
    |---|---|---|
    | Veil-render Seciae~ | Founding expansion of ☽'scar Ramtatus~ | Carried in ancestor-bone sheath; no recorded incident |
    | ☽'Netemia Glow-bringer | Still Pools blood-ward breaking | Fracture created; blade permanently damaged |
    | The Midnight Claws (Nightshelf syndicate) | Defense of ☽'slew Ravium~ | Wielded by Vorolum~ Vigil against orca raider Gnarkzund Zaith |
    | Aishouwa the Frost-Bane (Aurora Stack warrior) | Promenade Ward crisis | Corruption cold-seep froze the sheath shut; blade could not be drawn |
    | Thung Thrakrek (orca warlord) | Theft | A loss measured in tunnels the Nightshelf no longer holds |
    | ☽'Iarlocias the Silent (Nightshelf mystic) | Recovery by theft | Gifted to The Communion |
    | The Communion (Nightshelf cult) | Current custodian | Stored in silk at ☽'Saetacaeia-Viamana~ |
    
    The Vorolum~ engagement merits detail. During the defense of ☽'slew Ravium~, a Nightshelf stronghold, Vorolum~ Vigil of the Midnight Claws drove the blade into orca raider Gnarkzund Zaith's flank. The fracture split wider; captured forge-light poured through and detonated Zaith's pressure-marks inward. The ancestor-bone hilt fused to Vorolum~'s blistered flippers from the heat and fell free only when it cooled. She could not grip the blade again. Those who have carried ☽'micseleia' report the same phenomenon: holding it too long makes one's flippers ache with someone else's victories. The summary describes this as the blade *choosing* its wielders. I note only that every wielder discovered the blade's real gift was not victory but its cost.
    
    ## Interaction with Corruption
    
    Corrupted ice in the tunnels has been observed withdrawing from the blade, as though something older than the taint recognized the weapon's purpose. Yet during the Promenade Ward crisis, corruption cold-seep from deep ice bled upward and froze the sheath shut before Aishouwa could draw. The same taint the weapon repels can seal it when it reaches the housing first. This contradiction is consistent with what the Provenance Register describes as a pattern of irreversible harm tied to breach-point corruption.
    
    ## Current Disposition
    
    The Communion keeps ☽'micseleia' wrapped in silk in the deep dark of ☽'Saetacaeia-Viamana~, a Communion sanctuary, as a symbol of Firekeeper∴Doctrine. Pilgrims ask to hold it. The Communion refuses. They say the dagger has earned its rest.
    
    It was drawn from silk at least once more — to serve as signatory instrument for the Accord of Beacon Light and Deep Fire at The Beacon Stack, where Gilt-Hand Yeaiu qi'Gleam of Holdborn and Crypt-Chancellor Silvcaeclerae-fawn~ of Weal' Clique~ each drew blood upon the blade. The seal split where the fracture ran. Both parties noted this with bitter precision. The fissure is wider for it.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771585324345_0", anchor: "Four practitioners have attempted restoration. None succeeded.", text: "Four is the number the Deep-Archive register chose to record. It is also, conveniently, the number that makes restoration look thoroughly attempted rather than barely tried. The register names no practitioners. I have learned not to trust counts from documents that withhold the countable.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771585324345_1", anchor: "a phrasing I recognize from Nightshelf record-keeping as meaning", text: "Forty years reading Nightshelf administrative code and this construction still makes me shake my head — with something closer to admiration than frustration, at this point. They tell you the exact dimensions of what they are hiding and nothing whatsoever about what it contains. Superior record-keeping. They simply keep it for themselves.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771585324345_2", anchor: "Vorolum~ Vigil of the Midnight Claws drove the blade into orca raider Gnarkzund Zaith's flank", text: "Into the flank. Into the pressure-marks specifically, detonating them inward. Standard Nightshelf combat doctrine targeted orca at joint-lines and gill-approaches. Pressure-marks are internal magical anatomy — knowing their location requires either communion-practice or the kind of cross-species education I documented in my Nanaiamis~ annotation: the Midnight Claws' undisclosed training program with captured orca practitioners. Vorolum~ served the Claws in the same period. She did not guess where to strike. The main text presents this as a dramatic moment. It was a trained one.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771585324345_3", anchor: "as though something older than the taint recognized the weapon's purpose", text: "'Recognized.' There it is — I attributed intent to the ice and let it stand in my own entry. The Foundation Depths show identical displacement patterns around fire-core objects with no martial purpose at all: heating elements, forge-waste, a children's toy I catalogued at stratum four. The ice does not recognize purpose. It reacts to thermal signatures. I knew this when I wrote the sentence and chose the more evocative phrasing anyway. The crooked smile is for myself.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771585324345_4", anchor: "Both parties noted this with bitter precision. The fissure is wider for it.", text: "Using a dying weapon to solemnize a treaty between civilizations that cannot survive without each other. Everyone present understood the metaphor. That is why they chose it. Diplomacy on the Berg has always preferred its symbols load-bearing.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
